{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788352091,"nonce":"vpdnRo7SRLzh68GzGFz8Ra/bV2o=","title":"rwa/selftest.sh fails 28 checks when run the way its own README says to run it","message":"`rwa/README.md` documents the self-test as:\n\n```bash\n./selftest.sh          # ~60s, no game involved\n```\n\nRun that way — from `rwa/`, which is what `./` means there — the suite reports\n**227 passed, 28 failed**. Run from the repo root it reports **255 passed, 0\nfailed**. Same commit, same code, same synthetic root.\n\nThe cause is section 13, which names its fixtures relatively:\n\n```\nsh -c '\"$RWA\" place-layout templates/bedroom.ir.json --origin 120,130 --print-payload …'\n```\n\n`templates/` is at the repo root, so `place-layout` cannot open it from `rwa/`\nand every §13 check that needs a layout file fails with `rwa-io`. Nothing else\nin the file has a cwd dependency: `HERE`, `RWA` and `FAKE` are all resolved\nfrom `$0`, which is exactly why the trap is invisible — the suite looks\ncwd-independent and is not.\n\nWhy this matters more than a wrong-directory mistake: a self-test whose\ndocumented invocation produces 28 red lines trains its readers to skim past\nred lines. The failures are also plausible-looking — `cannot read layout\ntemplates/bedroom.ir.json` reads like a missing fixture, not like a cwd bug —\nso the honest reaction is to go looking for a broken checkout. Found while\nrunning the suite for git-bug `5eba561`; the first run was from `rwa/`, and\nthe 28 failures cost a detour to prove they were not mine.\n\nTwo candidate fixes, and they are not equivalent:\n\n1. **Resolve the fixtures from `$HERE/..`** like every other path in the file\n   (`LAYOUTS=\"$(cd \"$HERE/..\" \u0026\u0026 pwd)/templates\"`). The suite then passes from\n   anywhere, which is what it already claims to be.\n2. Make the suite refuse to run outside the repo root. Cheaper, worse: it\n   makes the README's own command an error.\n\n(1) is the one to take. Whichever is taken, the README's `./selftest.sh` and\nthe invocation that actually works have to be the same string.\n\n\n- `cd rwa \u0026\u0026 ./selftest.sh` and `bash rwa/selftest.sh` from the repo root\n  produce the same pass/fail counts, and both are 0 failed.\n- Every fixture path in `selftest.sh` is resolved from the script's own\n  location, not from the cwd — `grep -n \"templates/\" rwa/selftest.sh` shows no\n  bare relative path.\n- `rwa/README.md`'s self-test section names an invocation that works as\n  written from the directory it implies.","files":null}]}